  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 135 PM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nThe main lobe of vorticity that provided the snow activity in \ncentral and eastern Missouri has continued to push off to the south \nand southeast this afternoon, with accumulating snowfall ending for \nmost of our eastern zones. We will remain on the backside of the \ncyclonic flow of the H7 low for the remainder of the day, therefore \nwill continue to see clouds, though some breaks may be possible in \neastern KS just before sunset. Pressure gradient will remain strong \nthrough the early parts of the evening, which may maintain wind \ngusts between 15-20 MPH through this evening.\n\nThere is a mid-level ridge axis that is trying to work its away from \nthe Central to Eastern Plains overnight into Friday morning, \npromoting an AVA regime into the area. If this produces enough \nsubsidence during the late evening and start of the overnight hours, \nalong with continued northwesterly flow, temperatures will drop into \nthe lower and mid 20s across most of the area, making for a cold \nstart to Friday morning. This will continue to shift eastward. \nleading to mostly clear skies for Friday. Adiabatic compression over \nthe Front Range and High Plains along with westerly 850mb flow \nshould lead to WAA by Friday afternoon in the lower Missouri River \nValley, helping to boost temperatures into the lower 50s. Our far \neastern zones in central Missouri with an inch or two of snow on the \nground may stay in the lower to mid 40s. Saturday, 850mb thermal \nridge passes through the area, which will send temperatures back \ninto the mid 50s. A few disturbances in the western CONUS will work \nto de-amplify mid-level ridging, leading to zonal flow at H5. \n\nLate Saturday and into early Sunday, mid-level short-wave and strong \nvort maxima move across the Central CONUS, developing a panhandle \nhook surface cyclone. While the center of this cyclone is currently \nprogged to remain southeast of the area, the cooler airmass \nnorthwest of it is expected to remain above freezing. Current \nensemble guidance shows favorable moisture transport with this \nsystem that completely wraps around the backside, and therefore \nleading to very likely rain activity (over 70 percent for large \nportion of the forecast area by Sunday). Current deterministic \nguidance keeps most of the instability in the warm sector away \nfrom Central Missouri, and therefore not looking at any thunder \npotential with this rainfall activity.\n\nFor next week, strong PV anomaly over the eastern Pacific is \nexpected to park a deep trough over the western CONUS, but amplifies \nmid-level flow east of the Rockies. This pushes a strong thermal \nridge into the Central CONUS, which could push temperatures back \ninto the 60s by the middle of next week. Toward the end of next week \nas that trough moves inland, would bring another opportunity for \nprecipitation into the lower Missouri River Valley.",
